"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,361\nThe Learning Brain. Series 2: Memory\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,435\nEpisode 12\n\n3\n00:00:07,515 --> 00:00:09,527\nDesigning for memory\n\n4\n00:00:12,327 --> 00:00:16,581\nOver twelve episodes, we followed a memory from the moment it is noticed,\n\n5\n00:00:16,892 --> 00:00:19,451\nto the moment it is recalled and rewritten.\n\n6\n00:00:20,013 --> 00:00:23,800\nThis last episode turns that life into a course design.\n\n7\n00:00:23,970 --> 00:00:26,088\nAt encoding, guard attention.\n\n8\n00:00:26,541 --> 00:00:28,620\nAsk for meaning, not just form.\n\n9\n00:00:28,921 --> 00:00:32,096\nHave students generate answers rather than copy them.\n\n10\n00:00:32,370 --> 00:00:35,242\nAnd connect new material to what they already know.\n\n11\n00:00:35,412 --> 00:00:36,516\nThen give it time.\n\n12\n00:00:36,980 --> 00:00:39,217\nMemories stabilise over hours and days,\n\n13\n00:00:39,237 --> 00:00:41,348\nand sleep does much of that work.\n\n14\n00:00:41,695 --> 00:00:43,380\nDo not schedule against it.\n\n15\n00:00:43,550 --> 00:00:45,287\nCome back after some forgetting.\n\n16\n00:00:45,810 --> 00:00:48,588\nIn a study of more than thirteen hundred people,\n\n17\n00:00:48,852 --> 00:00:56,729\nthe best gap before review grew with how long the memory had to last: about a fifth of the time for a test a few weeks away,\n\n18\n00:00:56,993 --> 00:00:59,695\nfalling to five to ten percent for a year.\n\n19\n00:00:59,865 --> 00:01:01,526\nMake retrieval the main event.\n\n20\n00:01:02,059 --> 00:01:05,530\nQuiz often, ask for recall before showing answers,\n\n21\n00:01:05,746 --> 00:01:07,288\nvary the cues and settings,\n\n22\n00:01:07,308 --> 00:01:09,338\nand correct errors with feedback.\n\n23\n00:01:09,508 --> 00:01:17,080\nA major review of ten study techniques rated just two as high utility: practice testing and spaced practice.\n\n24\n00:01:17,489 --> 00:01:20,770\nRereading and highlighting, the ones students use most,\n\n25\n00:01:20,947 --> 00:01:22,165\nwere rated low.\n\n26\n00:01:22,335 --> 00:01:23,133\nA caution.\n\n27\n00:01:23,532 --> 00:01:27,033\nMuch of this evidence comes from labs and simple materials.\n\n28\n00:01:27,489 --> 00:01:30,187\nClassroom studies mostly point the same way,\n\n29\n00:01:30,207 --> 00:01:34,343\nbut effects vary, so measure what changes in your own course.\n\n30\n00:01:34,513 --> 00:01:35,672\nFor your next course,\n\n31\n00:01:35,692 --> 00:01:36,637\npick one unit.\n\n32\n00:01:37,079 --> 00:01:38,897\nAdd a short weekly quiz,\n\n33\n00:01:39,002 --> 00:01:41,415\nmix earlier topics into later quizzes,\n\n34\n00:01:41,624 --> 00:01:44,228\nand swap one review session for retrieval.\n\n35\n00:01:44,636 --> 00:01:46,893\nThen compare results with last year.\n\n36\n00:01:47,063 --> 00:01:48,998\nMemory is not where learning ends.\n\n37\n00:01:49,220 --> 00:01:50,940\nIt is how learning lasts.\n\n"
